1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,712
A metal detector beeps at
a coin hidden in a bag.

2
00:00:03,712 --> 00:00:06,653
Breathe into lime water,
and it turns milky.

3
00:00:07,053 --> 00:00:09,640
Something hidden gives
a signal you can see.

4
00:00:10,090 --> 00:00:11,982
Aarav's bicycle tube is flat.

5
00:00:11,982 --> 00:00:14,087
The shop man dips it in water.

6
00:00:14,087 --> 00:00:15,931
Bubbles rise from one spot.

7
00:00:16,281 --> 00:00:18,225
The hole was too small to see.

8
00:00:18,225 --> 00:00:19,838
The bubbles gave it away.

9
00:00:20,338 --> 00:00:22,590
Chemists find hidden
elements the same way.

10
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:25,138
By the end, you will
read four colour signals.

11
00:00:25,788 --> 00:00:28,270
An organic compound always has carbon.

12
00:00:28,270 --> 00:00:30,446
Which other elements hide inside?

13
00:00:30,446 --> 00:00:33,176
Finding out is called
qualitative analysis.

14
00:00:33,526 --> 00:00:36,704
Qualitative means what
is there, not how much.

15
00:00:37,104 --> 00:00:39,017
First, carbon and hydrogen.

16
00:00:39,017 --> 00:00:41,650
Heat the compound with copper two oxide.

17
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:44,029
Carbon becomes carbon dioxide.

18
00:00:44,029 --> 00:00:45,868
It turns lime water milky.

19
00:00:46,268 --> 00:00:47,688
Hydrogen becomes water.

20
00:00:47,688 --> 00:00:50,357
It turns white anhydrous
copper sulphate blue.

21
00:00:50,957 --> 00:00:53,304
Nitrogen, sulphur and halogens are harder.

22
00:00:53,304 --> 00:00:57,338
In the compound, they are held by shared
electrons, so test solutions cannot

23
00:00:57,338 --> 00:00:58,238
reach them.

24
00:00:58,506 --> 00:01:01,032
So we melt the compound with sodium metal.

25
00:01:01,032 --> 00:01:04,049
This is Lassaigne's test,
also called sodium fusion.

26
00:01:04,649 --> 00:01:07,214
Let's watch it on our molecule bench.

27
00:01:07,214 --> 00:01:10,142
Sodium gives away one
electron very easily.

28
00:01:10,492 --> 00:01:13,754
Carbon and nitrogen take
that electron together.

29
00:01:13,754 --> 00:01:15,720
They become a cyanide ion.

30
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:16,910
Sodium cyanide.

31
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:19,161
Sulphur takes two electrons.

32
00:01:19,161 --> 00:01:20,307
Sodium sulphide.

33
00:01:20,307 --> 00:01:21,766
A halogen takes one.

34
00:01:21,766 --> 00:01:22,814
Sodium halide.

35
00:01:23,214 --> 00:01:26,054
Now they are ions, and
ions dissolve in water.

36
00:01:26,054 --> 00:01:28,351
Boil the melt in water and filter it.

37
00:01:28,351 --> 00:01:30,408
This is the sodium fusion extract.

38
00:01:31,008 --> 00:01:32,189
Test for nitrogen.

39
00:01:32,189 --> 00:01:35,785
Add iron two sulphate, boil,
then add strong sulphuric acid.

40
00:01:36,185 --> 00:01:39,290
Cyanide wraps around iron
and makes a complex ion.

41
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:41,423
Some iron two becomes iron three.

42
00:01:41,823 --> 00:01:44,313
Together they make a deep blue solid.

43
00:01:44,313 --> 00:01:46,230
Prussian blue means nitrogen.

44
00:01:46,830 --> 00:01:48,150
Test for sulphur.

45
00:01:48,150 --> 00:01:50,481
Add acetic acid and lead acetate.

46
00:01:50,481 --> 00:01:53,648
A black solid, lead
sulphide, means sulphur.

47
00:01:54,047 --> 00:01:55,602
Or add sodium nitroprusside.

48
00:01:55,602 --> 00:01:57,544
A violet colour also means sulphur.

49
00:01:58,144 --> 00:01:59,302
Test for halogens.

50
00:01:59,302 --> 00:02:01,533
Add nitric acid, then silver nitrate.

51
00:02:01,933 --> 00:02:04,606
A white solid that dissolves
in ammonia means chlorine.

52
00:02:05,006 --> 00:02:08,497
Pale yellow, dissolving only
a little, means bromine.

53
00:02:08,897 --> 00:02:12,082
Yellow, not dissolving
at all, means iodine.

54
00:02:12,682 --> 00:02:14,792
Phosphorus is found another way.

55
00:02:14,792 --> 00:02:17,304
Heat the compound with sodium peroxide.

56
00:02:17,304 --> 00:02:19,149
Phosphorus becomes phosphate.

57
00:02:19,549 --> 00:02:23,003
Boil with nitric acid, and
add ammonium molybdate.

58
00:02:23,003 --> 00:02:25,877
A yellow colour or solid means phosphorus.

59
00:02:26,477 --> 00:02:28,009
Compare two extracts.

60
00:02:28,009 --> 00:02:31,290
Nitrogen alone gives
cyanide, and Prussian blue.

61
00:02:31,690 --> 00:02:34,978
Nitrogen and sulphur together
can give thiocyanate.

62
00:02:34,978 --> 00:02:39,756
With iron three, it turns blood red,
and Prussian blue does not show.

63
00:02:40,256 --> 00:02:41,297
Pause and think.

64
00:02:41,297 --> 00:02:43,699
Why do we use extra sodium in the fusion?

65
00:02:46,899 --> 00:02:50,782
Extra sodium breaks thiocyanate
into cyanide and sulphide.

66
00:02:50,782 --> 00:02:53,145
Then both tests show up clearly.

67
00:02:53,795 --> 00:02:54,744
Remember this.

68
00:02:54,744 --> 00:02:58,202
Sodium turns hidden atoms into
ions, and ions give colours.

69
00:02:58,902 --> 00:03:00,593
Now, one worked example.

70
00:03:00,893 --> 00:03:03,084
An extract gives no Prussian blue.

71
00:03:03,084 --> 00:03:05,409
With nitroprusside, it turns violet.

72
00:03:05,409 --> 00:03:09,650
After boiling with nitric acid, silver
nitrate gives a yellow solid that

73
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:11,548
does not dissolve in ammonia.

74
00:03:12,048 --> 00:03:13,256
No Prussian blue.

75
00:03:13,256 --> 00:03:14,369
So no nitrogen.

76
00:03:15,119 --> 00:03:16,250
Violet with nitroprusside.

77
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:17,224
So sulphur is there.

78
00:03:17,974 --> 00:03:20,188
Yellow solid, not dissolving.

79
00:03:20,188 --> 00:03:21,751
So iodine is there.

80
00:03:22,501 --> 00:03:26,330
The compound has sulphur and
iodine, along with its carbon.

81
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:28,441
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:03:28,791 --> 00:03:31,454
Students add silver nitrate
straight to the extract.

83
00:03:31,854 --> 00:03:36,049
But cyanide and sulphide also give
solids with silver, and fool you.

84
00:03:36,049 --> 00:03:38,893
Boil with nitric acid
first, to remove them.

85
00:03:39,543 --> 00:03:40,660
Where is this asked?

86
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:43,582
JEE Main has asked which elements
Lassaigne's test can detect.

87
00:03:43,582 --> 00:03:45,814
Boards ask for the
colours and the reactions.

88
00:03:46,414 --> 00:03:47,553
Exam level now.

89
00:03:47,553 --> 00:03:52,117
A sodium fusion extract, made with
little sodium, gives a blood red colour

90
00:03:52,117 --> 00:03:53,304
with iron three.

91
00:03:53,304 --> 00:03:55,137
Which compound could it be?

92
00:03:55,137 --> 00:03:58,823
Thiourea, urea, benzene
sulphonic acid, or chlorobenzene?

93
00:03:58,823 --> 00:04:00,010
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:05,410 --> 00:04:06,952
The answer is A.

95
00:04:06,952 --> 00:04:07,861
Thiourea.

96
00:04:08,361 --> 00:04:10,306
Blood red means thiocyanate.

97
00:04:10,306 --> 00:04:13,425
So the compound has both
nitrogen and sulphur.

98
00:04:13,925 --> 00:04:15,265
Thiourea has both.

99
00:04:15,265 --> 00:04:16,765
Urea has no sulphur.

100
00:04:17,265 --> 00:04:19,696
Benzene sulphonic acid has no nitrogen.

101
00:04:19,696 --> 00:04:21,339
Chlorobenzene has neither.

102
00:04:21,939 --> 00:04:23,480
Here is your recap.

103
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:25,062
Screenshot this card.

104
00:04:28,262 --> 00:04:30,448
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

105
00:04:30,848 --> 00:04:31,748
One.

106
00:04:31,313 --> 00:04:33,656
Why is the compound fused with sodium?

107
00:04:34,456 --> 00:04:35,356
Two.

108
00:04:34,906 --> 00:04:36,530
What colour shows nitrogen?

109
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:38,230
Three.

110
00:04:37,872 --> 00:04:41,802
Which halogen gives a yellow solid
that does not dissolve in ammonia?

111
00:04:42,702 --> 00:04:46,844
Next time: we know what is
inside, but how much of each?

112
00:04:46,844 --> 00:04:49,411
We find out in quantitative analysis.
